\begin_layout Title
Quiz 1: KKT Regimes in a Quantity-Quality Household
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
ECON 803 - Microeconomic Theory I - Fall 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 2, 2026 - 25 minutes - 20 points
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Name: ............................................................ Student number: ........................................
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Show the KKT conditions and enough algebra to identify every active set.
 The model and all notation needed for the quiz are defined below;
 no prior knowledge of Galor is required.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
A static household quantity-quality problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a household with 20 units of income.
 It allocates resources among its own consumption,
 the number of children,
 and investment in the children's human capital.
 More children increase family size (quantity),
 while more investment per child increases child quality.
 Because both uses are costly,
 the household faces a quantity-quality trade-off.
 Galor studies this mechanism inside a dynamic growth model;
 here we use a deliberately simple static problem to isolate the KKT economics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The choice variables are:
 
\begin_inset Formula $c\geq0$
\end_inset

 is parental consumption (the numeraire,
 with price one);
 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq0$
\end_inset

 is a divisible fertility index;
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H\geq0$
\end_inset

 is total human-capital investment across all children,
 not investment per child.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $n>0$
\end_inset

,
 average quality per child is
 
\begin_inset Formula $q=H/n$
\end_inset

.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1/4$
\end_inset

 means a near-zero positive value of this index,
 not literally one-quarter of a child.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The parameters are:
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 the direct resource cost of each unit of fertility;
 
\begin_inset Formula $k\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 the linear price of total human-capital investment;
 
\begin_inset Formula $s\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 the minimum required quality per child;
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline n_r\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 the fertility floor in regime 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

.
 The household chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $c,H\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq\underline n_r$
\end_inset

 to maximize
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
U(c,n,H)=c+6n-\frac12n^2+4H-\frac12H^2
\]
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The terms 
\begin_inset Formula $6n-n^2/2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $4H-H^2/2$
\end_inset

 give diminishing marginal benefits from family size and total child human capital.
 The quadratic specification is chosen for tractability,
 not as a literal description of preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The constraints are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
c+pn+kH+\frac12H^2\leq20,
\qquad
sn\leq H\leq4n,
\qquad
n\geq\underline n_r.
\]
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Budget:
 income is normalized to 20.
 Expenditure is parental consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

,
 direct child cost 
\begin_inset Formula $pn$
\end_inset

,
 linear education cost 
\begin_inset Formula $kH$
\end_inset

,
 and the convex cost 
\begin_inset Formula $H^2/2$
\end_inset

.
 The last term makes additional aggregate investment progressively more expensive.
 Since more 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 always raises utility,
 no income is discarded at an optimum.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Quality cone:
 
\begin_inset Formula $sn\leq H\leq4n$
\end_inset

 links total investment to the number of children.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $n>0$
\end_inset

,
 division by 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula $s\leq q=H/n\leq4$
\end_inset

.
 Thus every child must receive at least quality 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

,
 while 4 is a normalized upper bound on quality per child.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $n=0$
\end_inset

,
 the two inequalities force 
\begin_inset Formula $H=0$
\end_inset

,
 so the household cannot purchase child human capital without children.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fertility floor:
 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq\underline n_r$
\end_inset

 is zero in the first two regimes.
 In the last regime it is 
\begin_inset Formula $1/4$
\end_inset

,
 which produces a positive but near-zero KKT corner.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
1. General KKT system [8 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Use multipliers 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda,\mu_c,\mu_n,\alpha,\beta\geq0$
\end_inset

 for,
 respectively,
 the budget slack,
 
\begin_inset Formula $c\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $n-\underline n_r\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H-sn\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $4n-H\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Write the Lagrangian and the complete KKT system.
 [6]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Explain why these conditions are sufficient.
 Show that whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $c^*>0$
\end_inset

,
 the budget binds and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^*=1$
\end_inset

.
 [2]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2. Solve three regimes [9 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each regime,
 find 
\begin_inset Formula $(c^*,n^*,H^*)$
\end_inset

,
 report 
\begin_inset Formula $q^*=H^*/n^*$
\end_inset

 when it is defined,
 identify the active inequalities,
 and give one valid multiplier vector.
 You may first use the binding budget to eliminate consumption.
 [3 points each]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Quantity-dominant (Malthusian): 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,k,s,\underline n_r)=(2,5,0,0)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Modern quantity-quality balance: 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,k,s,\underline n_r)=(4,2,0,0)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
High-quality-cost,
 low-fertility (post-modern) extension: 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,k,s,\underline n_r)=(4,6,2,1/4)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3. Interpretation [3 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Describe the movement from quantity to quality across your first two solutions.
 [1]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Explain why a high quality price 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 alone need not eliminate fertility,
 but a high 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 together with the minimum-quality requirement 
\begin_inset Formula $H\geq2n$
\end_inset

 can push fertility down to its small lower bound.
 [1]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) A full growth model would let today's household choices affect tomorrow.
 Name one plausible feedback---for example,
 education could raise the next generation's human capital,
 fertility could change future population,
 or technology could change the return to education.
 Then explain why the binding low-fertility floor is our teaching extension rather than Galor's prediction.
 [1]
\end_layout

